From: Domenico D'Arrigo <d.darrigo@outlook.it>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 21:17:31 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] arch/x86: A coreboot table of 64 KiB

The coreboot table of x86 has 32 KiB. With DRIVERS_OPTION_CFR the setup
menu is one record of it, with the name and the help text of every
option: 28.2 KiB on the ThinkPad W541 with its 70 options, 26.9 KiB of
table on the machine before the last ten. Help texts were already
shortened twice to add an option, and write_tables() does not stop a
table that outgrows its CBMEM entry: it writes past the entry, over the
one added before it, and reports the size afterwards.

Double it. The tables written do not change, only the size of the
CBMEM entry they are written to; the payloads read the size of the
table from its header.

TEST=Build of the ThinkPad W541 with the native RAM init and of the
ThinkCentre M920x.
---
 src/arch/x86/include/arch/cbconfig.h | 8 +++++++-
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/arch/x86/include/arch/cbconfig.h b/src/arch/x86/include/arch/cbconfig.h
index 3779646e..84060a1d 100644
--- a/src/arch/x86/include/arch/cbconfig.h
+++ b/src/arch/x86/include/arch/cbconfig.h
@@ -10,6 +10,12 @@
  * to have the same name across all architectures.
  */
 
-#define COREBOOT_TABLE_SIZE 0x8000
+/*
+ * 64 KiB: with DRIVERS_OPTION_CFR the setup menu is one record of the table,
+ * with the name and the help text of every option (28 KiB on a board with 70
+ * of them), and write_tables() does not stop a table that outgrows its CBMEM
+ * entry: it writes past it and says so afterwards.
+ */
+#define COREBOOT_TABLE_SIZE 0x10000
 
 #endif
